{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# The defect\n\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh` resolves the two newest cap scripts as siblings of the\nRUNNING probe, using their REPOSITORY basename:\n\n```\n 999: VARTMP_CAP_SH=\"${VARTMP_CAP_SH:-$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)/vartmp-cap.sh}\"\n1092: CONTAINER_CAP_SH=\"${CONTAINER_CAP_SH:-$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)/container-cap.sh}\"\n```\n\n`install-observability.sh:12` installs the probe as `/usr/local/bin/rebar-observability.sh`,\nso `dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\"` is `/usr/local/bin` and those expand to\n`/usr/local/bin/vartmp-cap.sh` and `/usr/local/bin/container-cap.sh`.\n\n**Nothing ever creates those two paths.** `install-observability.sh` installs ONLY\n`observability.sh`. The scripts' own `--install` self-installs under a DIFFERENT,\n`rebar-`-prefixed name:\n\n```\ninfra/scripts/vartmp-cap.sh:121    VAR_TMP_INSTALLED_PATH=\"${...:-/usr/local/bin/rebar-vartmp-cap.sh}\"\ninfra/scripts/container-cap.sh:124 CONTAINER_INSTALLED_PATH=\"${...:-/usr/local/bin/rebar-container-cap.sh}\"\n```\n\nSo the name the probe looks for and the name the installer writes can never be the same file.\nThis is a naming mismatch, not a missing deploy step.\n\n## Proven on the host, both before and after the reapers were installed\n\nOn i-00880b2c7f13527c5 the probe's two invocations failed with **rc 127**:\n\n```\nbash: /usr/local/bin/vartmp-cap.sh: No such file or directory     vartmp_printenv_rc=127\nbash: /usr/local/bin/container-cap.sh: No such file or directory  container_printenv_rc=127\n```\n\n1. **Two cap-compliance percentages are never published.** `--print-env` produces no output, so\n   `VAR_TMP_MAX_BYTES` / `CONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES` stay unset and the budget guards at\n   `observability.sh:1037` and `:1118` are false. `var_tmp_used_percent` and\n   `container_writable_used_percent` are absent from the `rebar/host` namespace entirely, and\n   `rebar-var-tmp-usage-high` / `rebar-container-writable-usage-high` had **0 datapoints in 3h**.\n\n2. **Both heartbeats are pinned to 0 regardless of the true state.** `--check-active` returns an\n   empty string, which `case` coerces to `0`, so `var_tmp_cleanup_active` and\n   `container_reaper_active` publish 0 whether or not a reaper runs. Both dead-man alarms read\n   12/12 datapoints at 0.00.\n\nThe decisive evidence is what happened after `vartmp-cap.sh --install` and\n`container-cap.sh --install` were run on the host at 2026-09-06 01:23Z. Both reaper timers are\nnow in force, and the scripts answer 1 at their INSTALLED names — while the names the probe\nactually calls still do not exist:\n\n```\n# truth, via the installed (prefixed) path\nvartmp_true=[1]      container_true=[1]\n# what the probe calls\nbash: /usr/local/bin/vartmp-cap.sh: No such file or directory\nbash: /usr/local/bin/container-cap.sh: No such file or directory\n```\n\nSo a host with BOTH reapers correctly installed still publishes `0` for both heartbeats and\nstill omits both percentages. Repairing the host does not repair the metric; only this\nresolution change does.\n\nThe other two cap scripts are a natural control. `docker-storage-cap.sh` and `journald-cap.sh`\nare present at `/usr/local/bin` (neither defines an `INSTALLED_PATH`, so their repo basename is\nthe only name they have), and their percentages — `docker_storage_used_percent`,\n`journal_used_percent` — publish normally. Presence at the probe-sibling path is exactly what\nseparates the working pair from the broken pair, 4 for 4.\n\nExisting tests do not catch this because `tests/scripts/test_observability_var_tmp.py` runs\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh` in place, where the repo sibling `vartmp-cap.sh` IS present.\nThe test layout is the checkout layout and never the installed layout.\n\n## What this is hiding\n\n`/var/tmp` measured 3664 MiB against the 4 GiB budget `--print-env` reports — about **89%**,\nabove the 85% threshold of `rebar-var-tmp-usage-high`. The alarm that cannot see its metric\nwould be firing on a true condition if it could.\n\n## Not the cause\n\nThe probe is CURRENT: `sha256(/usr/local/bin/rebar-observability.sh)` equals\n`sha256(origin/main infra/scripts/observability.sh)` = `6d900dbde913...`, so the truncation fix\n`aaa31f11f3bc` is deployed. `probe_ok` and `probe_truncated` publish normally, so this is\nneither probe staleness nor truncation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `observability.sh` resolves each cap script through an ordered candidate list that\n      includes the name its own `--install` writes (`/usr/local/bin/rebar-vartmp-cap.sh`,\n      `/usr/local/bin/rebar-container-cap.sh`) and the repo-sibling name\n      (`vartmp-cap.sh`, `container-cap.sh`), picking the first that exists, so the installed\n      host and the checkout both resolve. Checked by\n      `pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_var_tmp.py tests/scripts/test_observability_container_layers.py`.\n- [ ] The `VARTMP_CAP_SH` / `CONTAINER_CAP_SH` environment overrides still take precedence over\n      every candidate, so tests injecting a stub path are unaffected. Checked by the existing\n      cases in the two suites above, which set those variables and must still pass.\n- [ ] A regression test proves the installed-name layout resolves: with only\n      `rebar-vartmp-cap.sh` beside the probe and no `vartmp-cap.sh`, a probe run publishes\n      `var_tmp_used_percent` and `var_tmp_cleanup_active=1`; the equivalent holds for\n      `container_writable_used_percent` and `container_reaper_active`. Reverting the resolution\n      change must fail this test.\n- [ ] A test proves the coupling cannot silently break again: the candidate list in\n      `observability.sh` contains the `*_INSTALLED_PATH` default declared in `vartmp-cap.sh`\n      and `container-cap.sh`, so renaming an installed path without updating the probe fails\n      the build. Checked by that test failing when either default is edited in isolation.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_var_tmp.py -x` — the /var/tmp probe suite, extended\n  with the installed-name layout case.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_observability_container_layers.py -x` — same for the writable\n  container-layer probe.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_vartmp_cap.py tests/scripts/test_container_cap.py -x` — proves the\n  `*_INSTALLED_PATH` defaults this change couples to are unchanged.\n- `make lint && make typecheck` — the commit gate.\n- Post-deploy verification on the host: after the change lands and autodeploy re-materializes\n  the probe, `aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace rebar/host --metric-name\n  var_tmp_used_percent` returns datapoints, and `var_tmp_cleanup_active` reads 1 rather than 0.\n\n## Out of scope (filed separately)\n\nThat `docker-storage-cap.sh` / `journald-cap.sh` are present at `/usr/local/bin` only by manual\noperator copy — no repo path installs them there — is tracked on\n`kittenish-venomous-pittabird` (`0bdf-b496-7201-4c84`)."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788657907884562373,"uuid":"be7ac744-1e03-4475-939d-c394d37c2083"}